/* TIH — generated lesson notes for a single course.
   Split out of the 909 KB lesson-content.js, which the course player used
   to load in full for every lesson: opening AgriTech fetched the notes for
   all 24 courses. tih-course-loader.js now fetches only this one. */
window.LESSON_CONTENT = window.LESSON_CONTENT || {};
window.LESSON_CONTENT['android'] = {"0":"<p>In this lesson, you will explore the Android ecosystem and set up Android Studio on your computer. Understanding how Android works as a platform is the foundation for everything you will build throughout this course. For students in Liberia and across West Africa, learning Android development is one of the most valuable technical skills you can acquire, Android powers over 90% of smartphones used across Africa, meaning the apps you build will reach millions of people right here on the continent.</p><p>The <strong>Android ecosystem</strong> refers to the entire world built around the Android operating system, developed by Google and running on billions of devices worldwide. Android is open source, meaning developers can build and distribute apps freely. The ecosystem includes the Android OS itself, the Google Play Store, Android Studio (the official development tool), and millions of developers contributing libraries and tools. Android versions such as Android 14 and Android 15 add new features and security improvements with each release that developers can take advantage of.</p><p><strong>Android Studio</strong> is the Integrated Development Environment (IDE) made specifically for building Android apps. Think of it as a workshop where all your tools, code editor, device emulator, debugger, and file manager, live in one place. Installing Android Studio also installs the <strong>Android SDK</strong> (Software Development Kit), which is the collection of tools and libraries that let your code communicate with phone hardware like cameras, GPS, and touchscreens. The built-in emulator lets you run and test your app on a virtual phone directly on your computer screen, so you do not need a physical device to begin coding.</p><p>Imagine a Liberian entrepreneur in Monrovia who wants to build a mobile app to manage orders for her fabric business at Waterside Market. She needs an app that runs on the affordable Android phones her customers already own. Android Studio gives her the complete toolkit to design her screens, write her logic in Kotlin, and test everything before publishing. The same tools used by Google engineers are free for students in Monrovia, Gbarnga, or Harper to use today.</p><ul><li><strong>Android Ecosystem:</strong> The complete platform including the OS, Play Store, SDK, and developer community surrounding Android devices.</li><li><strong>Android Studio:</strong> The official free IDE from Google used to design, code, and test Android applications.</li><li><strong>SDK (Software Development Kit):</strong> A package of tools, libraries, and APIs that allow your code to interact with Android hardware and system features.</li><li><strong>Emulator:</strong> A virtual Android device that runs on your computer, allowing you to test apps without a physical phone.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, download and install Android Studio on your computer, create a new empty project called HelloLiberia, and confirm that the emulator launches and displays the default blank app screen.</p>","1":"<p>This lesson introduces Kotlin, the modern programming language used to build Android apps. Kotlin is now the officially recommended language for Android development by Google, replacing older Java-based approaches. For Liberian learners, mastering Kotlin is like learning the grammar of a new language, once you understand the rules, you can express any idea as a working app. Every professional Android developer on the continent uses these exact same concepts daily.</p><p>A <strong>variable</strong> is a named container that holds a piece of data your program needs to remember. In Kotlin, you declare variables using two keywords: <strong>val</strong> for values that never change (like a user's date of birth) and <strong>var</strong> for values that can change over time (like a running total in a market app). Kotlin is also strongly typed, meaning every variable has a specific <strong>data type</strong>, for example, <em>Int</em> for whole numbers, <em>String</em> for text, <em>Double</em> for decimal numbers, and <em>Boolean</em> for true/false values. Declaring the correct type prevents many common bugs before your app even runs.</p><p>A <strong>function</strong> is a reusable block of code that performs a specific task. You define a function once using the <em>fun</em> keyword in Kotlin, and then call it whenever you need that task done. For example, a function called <em>calculateChange(price: Double, paid: Double): Double</em> takes two numbers and returns the difference, useful in a mobile point-of-sale app used by traders at Red Light Market in Paynesville. <strong>Control flow</strong> with <em>if/else</em> statements and <em>when</em> expressions lets your app make decisions, such as showing a discount message when a customer buys more than five items.</p><p>Consider building a simple tip calculator for a restaurant in Sinkor, Monrovia. You would store the bill amount in a <em>var</em> called billAmount of type Double, write a function called <em>calculateTip</em> that takes the bill and a tip percentage as parameters, and use an <em>if</em> statement to apply a higher tip rate on weekends. These three building blocks, variables, functions, and logic, combine to create every feature in every Android app ever built.</p><ul><li><strong>val / var:</strong> Keywords to declare immutable (val) or mutable (var) variables in Kotlin.</li><li><strong>Data Types:</strong> The kind of data a variable holds, Int, String, Double, Boolean are the most common.</li><li><strong>Function:</strong> A named, reusable block of code defined with the fun keyword that can accept parameters and return a result.</li><li><strong>Control Flow:</strong> if/else and when expressions that allow your program to make decisions based on conditions.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, open the Kotlin playground (play.kotlinlang.org) and write a function called greetUser that takes a name as a String parameter and prints a welcome message that includes the name, then call it three times with different Liberian names.</p>","2":"<p>This lesson is where your Android journey becomes real, you will build and run your first complete app. You will create a simple app that displays the message 'Hello Liberia!' on screen, and in doing so you will learn how Android apps are structured, what an Activity is, and how the user interface (UI) is defined. For every developer in Liberia and West Africa, building that first working app is a milestone that proves the skill is within reach.</p><p>An <strong>Activity</strong> is the fundamental building block of any Android app. Each screen the user sees and interacts with is backed by an Activity, a Kotlin class that manages what is displayed and responds to user actions. Every Activity has a <strong>lifecycle</strong>, a series of states it moves through: it is created, started, resumed (visible and active), paused, stopped, and eventually destroyed. The most important lifecycle method is <em>onCreate()</em>, which runs when the screen first appears and is where you set up your UI and initial logic. Understanding the lifecycle prevents bugs like apps crashing when the user rotates the phone.</p><p>The <strong>UI (User Interface)</strong> of an Android app is defined in XML layout files. When you call <em>setContentView(R.layout.activity_main)</em> inside your Activity's <em>onCreate()</em> method, you are telling Android which XML layout file to display. The XML file describes the visual elements, text, buttons, images, and their positions on screen. Inside your Kotlin code, you connect to those visual elements using their IDs with <em>findViewById()</em> or the newer <strong>View Binding</strong> approach, which is cleaner and less error-prone.</p><p>A practical example: imagine building an app for a community announcement board in Buchanan, Grand Bassa County. The main screen (Activity) shows the town name and a welcome message. You define a TextView in XML with the text 'Welcome to Buchanan!' and an ID of <em>tvWelcome</em>. In your MainActivity Kotlin class, you find that TextView using its ID and can dynamically change its text based on the time of day, 'Good Morning, Buchanan!' before noon and 'Good Evening, Buchanan!' after. This simple example uses Activities, XML layouts, and Kotlin logic all together.</p><ul><li><strong>Activity:</strong> A single screen in an Android app, represented by a Kotlin class that manages the UI and user interactions for that screen.</li><li><strong>Lifecycle:</strong> The series of states (created, started, resumed, paused, stopped, destroyed) that an Activity moves through during its existence.</li><li><strong>XML Layout:</strong> A file written in XML that describes the visual structure and appearance of a screen in your app.</li><li><strong>setContentView():</strong> The method called in onCreate() that links your Activity to its XML layout file.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, modify your Hello Liberia app so that the displayed text changes to show your own name and county, for example, 'Hello from Nimba County!', by editing both the XML layout file and the MainActivity Kotlin class.</p>","4":"<p>This lesson dives into XML layouts, the system Android uses to describe what every screen looks like visually. XML layouts are where you design the buttons users tap, the text fields where they type, and the labels that display information. For developers in Liberia building real-world apps, whether for agriculture, mobile money, health, or education, mastering layouts is the skill that separates apps that look professional from apps that look unfinished.</p><p>In Android XML layouts, every visual element is called a <strong>View</strong>. The most commonly used Views are: <strong>TextView</strong>, which displays text the user reads but cannot edit; <strong>EditText</strong>, a text input field where users type information such as a name, phone number, or amount; and <strong>Button</strong>, a tappable element that triggers an action when pressed. Views are arranged inside <strong>ViewGroups</strong>, container elements that control how child views are positioned. The two most important ViewGroups are <strong>LinearLayout</strong>, which stacks views vertically or horizontally in a line, and <strong>ConstraintLayout</strong>, which allows you to position views precisely relative to each other and to the screen edges.</p><p>Every View in your XML needs key attributes to work correctly. The <strong>layout_width</strong> and <strong>layout_height</strong> attributes control the size, use <em>match_parent</em> to fill the available space or <em>wrap_content</em> to be only as large as the content inside. The <strong>id</strong> attribute gives each View a unique name so your Kotlin code can find and control it. The <strong>hint</strong> attribute on an EditText shows placeholder text (like 'Enter your phone number') before the user starts typing. The <strong>text</strong> attribute on a TextView or Button sets what is displayed.</p><p>Consider building a mobile money transfer form for an app used by Lonestar Cell MTN customers in Liberia. The screen needs a TextView label reading 'Recipient Number', an EditText where the sender types the phone number, another EditText for the amount in Liberian Dollars, and a Button labeled 'Send Money'. Using ConstraintLayout, you position each element clearly below the previous one with proper margins, making the form easy to use on both small and large screens. Proper layout design makes the difference between an app customers trust and one they delete.</p><ul><li><strong>View:</strong> Any single visual element in an Android layout, a piece of text, an input field, a button, or an image.</li><li><strong>TextView:</strong> A View that displays read-only text to the user on screen.</li><li><strong>EditText:</strong> A View that provides an input field where users can type text or numbers.</li><li><strong>ConstraintLayout:</strong> A powerful ViewGroup that positions Views relative to each other and screen edges, ideal for complex, flexible screen designs.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, design an XML layout for a simple registration form with a title TextView ('Create Account'), three EditText fields for name, phone number, and county, and a Button labeled 'Register', position all elements using ConstraintLayout.</p>","5":"<p>This lesson covers one of the most important skills in Android development: building apps with multiple screens and passing information between them. Almost every real app has more than one screen, a login screen, a home screen, a details screen. For Liberian developers building market apps, health apps, or educational platforms, knowing how to connect screens and share data is what makes your app feel complete and professional.</p><p>In Android, navigating from one screen to another is done using an <strong>Intent</strong>. An Intent is an object that represents a request to do something, in this case, to start a new Activity (screen). You create an Intent by specifying the current context and the Activity class you want to open: <em>Intent(this, DetailActivity::class.java)</em>. Then you call <em>startActivity(intent)</em> to launch it. This is called an <strong>Explicit Intent</strong> because you name the exact destination Activity. Android also supports <strong>Implicit Intents</strong>, where you describe what action you want (like opening a web URL or dialing a phone number) and Android picks the right app to handle it.</p><p>Passing <strong>data between screens</strong> is done by attaching extras to your Intent before launching it. You use <em>intent.putExtra('KEY', value)</em> to attach a piece of data, a String, Int, or Boolean, with a key name. In the receiving Activity, you retrieve that data using <em>intent.getStringExtra('KEY')</em> or the equivalent method for the data type. For example, when a user taps on a product in a list, you put the product name and price into the Intent as extras, and the detail screen reads those extras to display the right information. It is critical to use consistent key names, a common practice is to define them as constants in a companion object.</p><p>Imagine building a farmers market app for the Lofa County Agricultural Cooperative. The first screen shows a list of available crops (cassava, rice, palm oil). When the farmer taps 'Cassava', the app creates an Intent, attaches the crop name 'Cassava' and its current price '450 LD per bag' as extras, and opens the CropDetailActivity. That second screen reads those extras and displays the full information: price, availability, seller contact, and a button to place an order. Without Intent-based navigation, this two-screen interaction would be impossible.</p><ul><li><strong>Intent:</strong> An Android object that represents a request to perform an action, most commonly, starting a new Activity (screen).</li><li><strong>Explicit Intent:</strong> An Intent that directly names the destination Activity class to launch.</li><li><strong>Implicit Intent:</strong> An Intent that describes an action (open URL, dial number) and lets Android decide which app handles it.</li><li><strong>Extras:</strong> Key-value pairs of data attached to an Intent so information can be passed from one Activity to another.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, create a two-screen app where the first screen has an EditText and a Button labeled 'Go', and tapping the button opens a second screen that displays the text the user typed, use putExtra and getStringExtra to pass the data.</p>","6":"<p>This lesson introduces RecyclerView, the most powerful and efficient way to display lists and grids of data in Android. If your app needs to show a scrollable list of products, students, transactions, or news articles, RecyclerView is the tool you will use. For developers in Liberia building apps that handle real data, a school attendance app, a market inventory system, or a church membership list, understanding RecyclerView is essential for building apps that work smoothly even with hundreds of items.</p><p>A <strong>RecyclerView</strong> is efficient because it <em>recycles</em> the View objects that scroll off screen instead of creating new ones for every item. Imagine a list of 500 market products, without recycling, Android would create 500 separate layout objects in memory, causing the app to slow down or crash. RecyclerView only keeps enough View objects to fill the screen, reusing them as the user scrolls. This is managed through a class called the <strong>ViewHolder</strong>, which holds references to the Views inside each list item so they do not need to be looked up repeatedly with findViewById (an expensive operation).</p><p>To use RecyclerView, you must write an <strong>Adapter</strong>, a class that acts as the bridge between your data (a list of objects) and the RecyclerView's display. The Adapter has three critical methods you must implement: <em>onCreateViewHolder()</em> inflates (creates) the item layout XML and wraps it in a ViewHolder; <em>onBindViewHolder()</em> takes data from your list at a specific position and puts it into the ViewHolder's Views; and <em>getItemCount()</em> tells RecyclerView how many items are in your list. The <strong>LayoutManager</strong> controls whether items are arranged in a vertical list (<em>LinearLayoutManager</em>), a grid (<em>GridLayoutManager</em>), or a horizontal scroll.</p><p>Picture a student result app for a school in Kakata, Margibi County. The app stores a list of Student objects, each with a name and test score. A RecyclerView with a custom Adapter displays each student's name on the left and their score on the right in a neat row. Using GridLayoutManager, you could instead display student profile cards in a two-column grid. When the school grows from 50 students to 500, the RecyclerView handles the larger list with no performance change because of its recycling mechanism.</p><ul><li><strong>RecyclerView:</strong> An efficient, scrollable list/grid widget that recycles View objects to maintain smooth performance with large datasets.</li><li><strong>ViewHolder:</strong> A class that holds cached references to the Views inside a single list item, avoiding repeated and costly findViewById calls.</li><li><strong>Adapter:</strong> The class that connects your data list to the RecyclerView by creating ViewHolders and binding data to them.</li><li><strong>LayoutManager:</strong> Controls the visual arrangement of items, LinearLayoutManager for lists, GridLayoutManager for grids.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, build a RecyclerView that displays a hardcoded list of at least eight Liberian counties, showing each county name in a separate row with a simple list item layout, use LinearLayoutManager and a custom Adapter class.</p>","8":"<p>This lesson covers how to save and retrieve data on the user's device, a critical skill for building apps that remember information between sessions. Without local data storage, every time the user closes your app all their data disappears. For practical apps in Liberia, tracking daily sales at a shop, saving a student's quiz progress, or storing a user's language preference, local storage is what makes an app genuinely useful in everyday life.</p><p><strong>SharedPreferences</strong> is Android's simplest storage system, ideal for saving small amounts of data as key-value pairs, similar to a settings file. It is perfect for storing user preferences like a chosen theme (light or dark), a login session token, or a user's name after they first register. You write to SharedPreferences using an Editor: call <em>getSharedPreferences('MyPrefs', Context.MODE_PRIVATE)</em> to get a reference, then <em>edit().putString('username', 'Korto').apply()</em> to save. To read back, call <em>prefs.getString('username', 'Guest')</em>, where 'Guest' is the default value returned if nothing was saved yet. SharedPreferences data persists even after the app is closed and reopened.</p><p><strong>SQLite</strong> is a full relational database built into every Android device, used when your app needs to store structured data with multiple fields and the ability to query, sort, and filter. Android provides <strong>Room</strong>, a library that makes working with SQLite much simpler and safer by letting you define your database using Kotlin classes and annotations. You define an <strong>Entity</strong> (a data class annotated with <em>@Entity</em> that represents a database table), a <strong>DAO</strong> (Data Access Object, an interface with annotated methods like <em>@Insert</em>, <em>@Query</em>, <em>@Delete</em>), and a <strong>Database</strong> class that ties everything together. Room validates your SQL queries at compile time, catching errors before users ever see them.</p><p>Consider a mobile inventory app for a provisions store on Broad Street, Monrovia. The owner needs to track product names, quantities, and prices. Using Room, you define a Product entity with fields for id, name, quantity, and priceInLD. The DAO has methods like <em>getAllProducts()</em> and <em>updateQuantity()</em>. When the owner receives new stock, the app inserts a record; when she sells an item, the app updates the quantity. Even without an internet connection, which can be unreliable in many parts of Liberia, the app continues working perfectly because all data lives on the device.</p><ul><li><strong>SharedPreferences:</strong> A simple key-value storage system for saving small pieces of data like settings and user preferences that persist between app sessions.</li><li><strong>SQLite:</strong> A lightweight relational database built into Android, suitable for storing structured data in tables with rows and columns.</li><li><strong>Room:</strong> Google's recommended library that provides a clean Kotlin-based abstraction layer over SQLite, with compile-time query validation.</li><li><strong>DAO (Data Access Object):</strong> An interface in Room that defines the methods (insert, query, update, delete) used to interact with the database.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, create a simple Room database for a contacts app with a Contact entity containing id, name, and phoneNumber fields, implement a DAO with insert and getAllContacts methods, and test that saving and retrieving a contact works correctly.</p>","9":"<p>This lesson teaches you how to connect your Android app to the internet to fetch and send real data from web services (APIs). The ability to connect to the internet transforms your app from a standalone tool into a live, data-driven product. For developers in Liberia and West Africa, this skill is especially powerful, it enables apps that show live exchange rates between Liberian Dollars and USD, real-time weather in Monrovia, or product listings from an online catalog, all updated automatically without republishing the app.</p><p>An <strong>API (Application Programming Interface)</strong> is a service on the internet that your app can send requests to and receive structured data back. Most modern APIs communicate using <strong>REST</strong>, a widely used standard where you make HTTP requests (GET to read data, POST to send data) to specific URLs called <strong>endpoints</strong>. The data returned is almost always formatted as <strong>JSON</strong> (JavaScript Object Notation), a lightweight text format that looks like: <em>{\"city\": \"Monrovia\", \"temp\": 28, \"condition\": \"Partly Cloudy\"}</em>. JSON uses curly braces for objects and square brackets for arrays, making it easy to represent complex data like a list of products or a weather forecast.</p><p><strong>Retrofit</strong> is the most widely used Android library for making API calls. You define a Kotlin interface where each method represents one API endpoint, annotated with <em>@GET</em> or <em>@POST</em> and the URL path. Retrofit handles the actual network communication and uses a converter (like <strong>Gson</strong> or <strong>Moshi</strong>) to automatically convert the JSON response into Kotlin data objects, this process is called <strong>deserialization</strong>. Because network calls take time and must not freeze the UI, Retrofit works with <strong>Coroutines</strong> (using the <em>suspend</em> keyword) or callbacks to run network requests in the background while the UI stays responsive.</p><p>Imagine building an app that shows current currency exchange rates for Liberian entrepreneurs who import goods. You call a free exchange rate API, receiving JSON with the current USD to LRD rate. Retrofit deserializes this into a Kotlin ExchangeRate data class. Your app then displays '1 USD = 182 LRD' on the screen, updated every time the user opens the app. Without Retrofit and JSON parsing, building this feature would require writing hundreds of lines of low-level networking code. Retrofit reduces it to about twenty lines.</p><ul><li><strong>API (Application Programming Interface):</strong> A web service that your app communicates with to send or receive data over the internet.</li><li><strong>JSON (JavaScript Object Notation):</strong> The standard text-based format used to structure and transmit data between apps and web services.</li><li><strong>Retrofit:</strong> A popular Android library that simplifies making HTTP requests to REST APIs and automatically converts JSON responses to Kotlin objects.</li><li><strong>Deserialization:</strong> The process of converting raw JSON text into usable Kotlin data class objects that your app can work with.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, use Retrofit to call the free public API at https://restcountries.com/v3.1/name/liberia, parse the JSON response to extract Liberia's population and capital city, and display both values in TextViews on your app's main screen.</p>","10":"<p>This lesson covers three advanced but essential Android capabilities: requesting device permissions, sending notifications to users, and running tasks in the background. These features are what allow apps to feel integrated into the phone rather than isolated from it. For app developers in Liberia building health reminder apps, community alert systems, or field data collection tools, these capabilities are the difference between an app users engage with daily and one that gets forgotten.</p><p>Android uses a <strong>permissions</strong> system to protect users' privacy and device resources. Before your app can access sensitive features like the camera, microphone, location, or contacts, it must declare the permission in the <em>AndroidManifest.xml</em> file and, for dangerous permissions, also request approval from the user at runtime using <em>ActivityResultContracts.RequestPermission()</em>. The user sees a dialog saying 'Allow this app to access your location?' and can accept or deny. Your code must always handle both outcomes gracefully, if the user denies, the app should explain why the permission is needed and offer an alternative path, not simply crash.</p><p><strong>Notifications</strong> are messages displayed in the phone's notification bar, even when the app is not open. They are created using a <strong>NotificationCompat.Builder</strong>, which lets you set a title, body text, small icon, and the action to take when the user taps the notification (typically opening a specific Activity). Since Android 8.0 (Oreo), notifications must be assigned to a <strong>Notification Channel</strong>, a category that users can manage individually in their phone settings, such as 'Reminders' or 'Alerts'. <strong>Background Tasks</strong> that need to run even when the app is closed, like syncing data or sending a scheduled reminder, are handled using <strong>WorkManager</strong>, Android's recommended library for reliable background work that survives app restarts and even phone reboots.</p><p>Consider a maternal health reminder app for community health workers in Bong County, Liberia. The app needs location permission to log where a home visit occurred, sends a notification reminding the health worker to follow up with a patient in 48 hours, and uses WorkManager to run a background sync every morning that uploads the previous day's visit records to the clinic's server, even if the health worker forgot to open the app. Each of these three features relies on the concepts covered in this lesson.</p><ul><li><strong>Runtime Permissions:</strong> Dangerous permissions (camera, location, contacts) that must be explicitly approved by the user when the app requests them.</li><li><strong>Notification Channel:</strong> A category (required since Android 8.0) that groups notifications by type, allowing users to customize or silence specific categories.</li><li><strong>WorkManager:</strong> Android's library for scheduling reliable background tasks that run even when the app is closed, accounting for battery optimization and device restarts.</li><li><strong>AndroidManifest.xml:</strong> The configuration file where all app permissions, activities, and services must be declared before they can be used.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, build a simple app that requests the READ_CONTACTS permission at runtime, shows a notification with the title 'TIH Academy' and body 'Time to practice Android!' when a button is tapped, and correctly handles both the permission granted and permission denied cases.</p>","12":"<p>This lesson covers testing, debugging, and performance optimization, the professional practices that separate apps that work reliably from apps that crash, freeze, or drain battery. Many developers skip this stage and rush to publish, only to receive bad reviews and uninstalls. For developers in Liberia and West Africa building apps for communities that depend on them, shipping a stable, fast app is a matter of professional reputation and real-world impact.</p><p><strong>Debugging</strong> is the process of finding and fixing errors (bugs) in your code. Android Studio includes a powerful debugger that lets you set <strong>breakpoints</strong>, markers on specific lines of code where execution pauses so you can inspect variable values at that exact moment. The <strong>Logcat</strong> panel in Android Studio shows a live stream of log messages from your app, you add your own messages using <em>Log.d('TAG', 'message here')</em> to trace what your code is doing. When your app crashes, Logcat shows a <strong>stack trace</strong>, a detailed error report showing exactly which line caused the crash and why, making it the first place every developer looks when something goes wrong.</p><p><strong>Testing</strong> is writing code that automatically verifies your app works correctly. <strong>Unit tests</strong> (written with JUnit) test individual functions in isolation, for example, confirming that your <em>calculateDiscount()</em> function returns the correct value for ten different input combinations. <strong>Instrumented tests</strong> run on a real device or emulator and test the full UI behavior. <strong>Performance optimization</strong> involves using Android Studio's <strong>Profiler</strong> tool to measure CPU usage, memory consumption, and network activity in real time. Common optimizations include avoiding heavy work on the main (UI) thread, using lazy loading for images, and minimizing unnecessary object creation inside RecyclerView adapters.</p><p>Imagine a school management app used by 30 teachers at a school in Zwedru, Grand Gedeh County. Without testing, a bug in the attendance-saving function could silently record wrong values for weeks. A unit test would catch that bug immediately every time the code is built. If the app runs slowly on older Android phones, which are common in Liberia, the Profiler would reveal that images are being loaded on the main thread, and fixing that one issue could make the app twice as fast for every user on low-end devices.</p><ul><li><strong>Breakpoint:</strong> A marker set on a line of code in Android Studio that pauses execution so you can inspect the program's state at that moment.</li><li><strong>Logcat:</strong> Android Studio's real-time log viewer that shows system messages, your custom log statements, and crash stack traces.</li><li><strong>Unit Test:</strong> Automated code that tests a single function or class in isolation to verify it produces correct output for given inputs.</li><li><strong>Android Profiler:</strong> A tool inside Android Studio that monitors CPU, memory, and network usage in real time to identify performance bottlenecks.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, open any app you have built in this course, add three Log.d statements to trace the flow through your main Activity's lifecycle methods (onCreate, onStart, onResume), run the app, and confirm you can see those log messages appearing in the correct order in Logcat.</p>","13":"<p>This lesson introduces Material Design, Google's comprehensive design system for creating Android apps that look polished, professional, and trustworthy. The visual quality of your app directly affects whether users adopt and recommend it. For Liberian developers aiming to compete in a global marketplace, or simply to build apps their community will be proud to use, Material Design provides a proven, free design framework used by apps like Google Pay, Gmail, and thousands of successful apps worldwide.</p><p><strong>Material Design</strong> is built on a set of principles: apps should feel like physical materials with depth and shadows, motion should be meaningful and guide the user's attention, and components should be consistent across the entire app. Google provides the <strong>Material Components for Android</strong> library (MDC-Android), which you add to your project's build.gradle file and gives you access to pre-built, well-designed UI components. Key components include the <strong>MaterialButton</strong> (with proper padding, ripple effects, and elevation), <strong>TextInputLayout</strong> with floating labels that animate when the user taps an EditText, and <strong>CardView</strong> which displays content in elevated, rounded-corner cards.</p><p>A <strong>color theme</strong> is central to Material Design. You define your app's colors in the <em>res/values/themes.xml</em> file using Material's color system: <em>colorPrimary</em> (your main brand color), <em>colorSecondary</em> (an accent color), and <em>colorSurface</em> (the background color of cards and sheets). Changing these theme colors updates every component in your app simultaneously, making rebranding effortless. Material Design 3 (the latest version) also supports <strong>dynamic color</strong> on Android 12+, where the app's color scheme adapts to match the user's wallpaper, a feature that makes apps feel native to each user's phone.</p><p>Imagine redesigning a microfinance app used by savings groups (susus) in Margibi County. Before applying Material Design, the app used plain white backgrounds and default gray buttons. After applying a green colorPrimary (#1B5E20) to reflect prosperity and trust, replacing standard buttons with MaterialButtons that show a satisfying ripple when tapped, and wrapping loan summary data in CardViews with subtle shadows, the same app feels like a professional banking product. Group members trust it more, and the developer's reputation grows across the county.</p><ul><li><strong>Material Design:</strong> Google's design system that defines principles, components, and guidelines for building visually consistent and professional Android apps.</li><li><strong>MDC-Android (Material Components for Android):</strong> The official library providing ready-to-use Material Design UI components like buttons, text fields, cards, and navigation bars.</li><li><strong>Color Theme:</strong> A set of named colors defined in themes.xml (colorPrimary, colorSecondary, colorSurface) that apply consistently across the entire app.</li><li><strong>CardView:</strong> A Material component that displays content in a rounded-corner, elevated container, giving information a clean, organized appearance.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, take a screen layout you built earlier in this course and upgrade it using Material Components, replace at least one Button with a MaterialButton, wrap a section of content in a CardView, and define a custom colorPrimary in your theme using a color that represents your personal brand or your Liberian county's identity.</p>","14":"<p>This final lesson walks you through publishing your Android app to the Google Play Store so that anyone in the world can download it. Reaching this stage means you have built something real, a complete Android app created by a developer from Liberia. For students and developers across West Africa, publishing on the Play Store is a milestone that opens doors to freelance opportunities, job offers, investor attention, and the ability to generate income directly from your work.</p><p>Before publishing, you must prepare a <strong>release build</strong> of your app. During development, Android Studio generates <strong>debug builds</strong> that include extra debugging information and are signed with a generic debug key, these cannot be published. A release build is optimized, has debugging information stripped out, and must be signed with your own private <strong>keystore</strong>, a file that contains a cryptographic key proving the app was built by you. You generate this keystore once in Android Studio under Build > Generate Signed Bundle/APK, store it securely (losing it means you can never update your app on the same listing), and use it to sign every update forever. Google now requires apps to be published as <strong>Android App Bundles (.aab)</strong> rather than the older APK format, as bundles allow Google to optimize the download size for each device model.</p><p>Publishing on the <strong>Google Play Console</strong> (play.google.com/console) requires a one-time $25 USD registration fee for your developer account. Once registered, you create a new app listing and fill in: the app title, short and full descriptions, a category (Education, Business, Finance, etc.), content rating answers, and at minimum two high-quality screenshots. You upload your signed .aab file, complete the store listing, and submit for review. Google's automated and human review process typically takes a few hours to a few days. <strong>App Store Optimization (ASO)</strong>, choosing the right keywords in your title and description, using compelling screenshots, and maintaining a high rating through quick responses to reviews, determines whether users find and install your app over competing ones.</p><p>A Liberian university student builds a revision app for WAEC (West African Examinations Council) exam preparation, targeting students across Liberia, Sierra Leone, Ghana, and Nigeria. She publishes it on the Play Store with a clear title ('WAEC Prep 2025'), screenshots showing the question bank interface, and a description mentioning all four countries. Within a month, 2,000 students download the app. She adds a premium question pack for $0.99 using Google Play's in-app purchases and earns enough to pay her university tuition. This is the direct economic opportunity that publishing skills unlock.</p><ul><li><strong>Keystore:</strong> A secure file containing your unique private signing key, required to sign release builds and every future update to your app on the Play Store.</li><li><strong>Android App Bundle (.aab):</strong> The modern format for submitting apps to Google Play, which allows Play to deliver device-optimized APKs and reduce download sizes.</li><li><strong>Google Play Console:</strong> The web platform where developers manage their app listings, upload builds, review analytics, and respond to user feedback.</li><li><strong>ASO (App Store Optimization):</strong> The practice of optimizing your app's title, description, and screenshots to improve visibility and install rates on the Play Store.</li></ul><p><strong>Practice:</strong> After watching the video, create a Google Play developer account, generate a signed release App Bundle for one of the apps you built during this course, create a new internal testing track listing in the Play Console with a real description and screenshots, and upload your .aab file to that internal track.</p>","15":"<p>This quiz covers <strong>Module 4: Testing, Material Design & Publishing</strong>, debugging with Logcat and the emulator, unit testing, avoiding ANR freezes, Material Design components like the FAB and elevation, and the release pipeline: signing with a keystore, building an App Bundle, and completing your Play Store listing.</p><p>You need <strong>70% to pass</strong> and you have <strong>unlimited retries</strong>. These are the exact steps between your finished code and a live app real users can download, worth knowing cold. Review lessons 4.1 to 4.3 if needed, then go for it!</p>","16":"<p>You have built screens with XML layouts, a skill still used in thousands of existing apps. But Google's modern toolkit, <strong>Jetpack Compose</strong>, is now the recommended way to build Android UI, and job postings across Africa and worldwide increasingly ask for it. Learning Compose puts you ahead of many developers who trained only a few years ago, and it makes UI dramatically faster to build, a real advantage when you are shipping apps for Monrovia businesses on tight timelines.</p><h4>The Big Idea: Declarative UI</h4><p>With XML, you describe a layout, then write Kotlin to find views and mutate them. Compose flips this: you write Kotlin functions that <strong>declare what the UI should look like for a given state</strong>, and when the state changes, Compose automatically redraws the affected parts. Less code, fewer bugs, no findViewById.</p><h4>Composable Functions</h4><p>UI pieces are functions marked with <strong>@Composable</strong>. A greeting is simply: fun Greeting(name: String) with Text(\"Hello $name\") inside. You build screens by combining small composables:</p><ul><li><strong>Text, Button, Image, TextField</strong>, the basic building blocks.</li><li><strong>Column</strong> stacks children vertically; <strong>Row</strong> places them horizontally; <strong>Box</strong> overlaps them.</li><li><strong>Modifier</strong> chains styling and behavior: Modifier.padding(16.dp).fillMaxWidth() replaces many XML attributes.</li><li><strong>LazyColumn</strong> replaces RecyclerView, an efficient scrolling list in a few lines, with no adapter or ViewHolder classes at all.</li></ul><h4>State: The Heart of Compose</h4><p>Compose redraws when state changes. You create observable state with <strong>remember { mutableStateOf(0) }</strong>, for example a counter: clicking a Button increments the value, and the Text showing it updates automatically. This state-driven thinking is the single most important concept in the module: UI = a function of state.</p><h4>Trying It</h4><p>In Android Studio, create a new project with the \"Empty Activity\" (Compose) template. The preview pane renders composables live with @Preview, without launching an emulator, a big time-saver on modest hardware. Rebuild the \"Hello Liberia\" screen from Module 1 in Compose and compare: the XML version needed a layout file plus activity code; Compose does it in one short function.</p><p>Your XML knowledge is not wasted, you will maintain existing apps with it for years. But from this lesson forward, we build new screens the modern way, and in lesson 5.2 we connect them to professional app architecture.</p>","17":"<p>Small demo apps can keep all their code in one Activity. Real apps, a mobile money tracker, a school results portal, a delivery app for Monrovia, quickly become unmaintainable that way. Professional Android teams organize code with <strong>MVVM (Model-View-ViewModel)</strong> architecture, and they handle slow work like network calls with <strong>coroutines</strong>. These two topics are the most common Android interview subjects after the basics, so this lesson is directly career-relevant.</p><h4>MVVM: Three Clear Responsibilities</h4><ul><li><strong>Model:</strong> your data and its sources, API services (Retrofit from Module 3), databases, and repository classes that decide where data comes from.</li><li><strong>View:</strong> your UI, composables or XML screens. It only displays state and forwards user actions. No business logic lives here.</li><li><strong>ViewModel:</strong> the bridge. It holds screen state, calls the repository, and survives configuration changes like screen rotation (as you saw in Module 2). The View observes the ViewModel's state and redraws when it changes.</li></ul><p>The payoff: when the UI changes, your logic is untouched; when the API changes, only the repository is affected. Teams can split work cleanly, and each layer can be tested alone.</p><h4>Coroutines: Doing Slow Work Without Freezing</h4><p>Remember ANR errors from Module 4, blocking the main thread freezes the app. Kotlin's answer is coroutines: lightweight background tasks written like normal sequential code.</p><ul><li>Mark slow functions with <strong>suspend</strong>, e.g., suspend fun getNews(): List&lt;Article&gt;.</li><li>Launch work from a ViewModel with <strong>viewModelScope.launch { }</strong>; it is automatically cancelled when the screen goes away, preventing leaks.</li><li>Wrap the result in a loading/success/error state so the UI can show a spinner, the data, or a friendly retry message, essential on Liberian mobile networks where requests fail often.</li></ul><h4>The Standard Flow</h4><p>User opens the screen; the ViewModel launches a coroutine; the repository fetches from the API; the ViewModel updates its state from loading to success; Compose redraws with the data. Every professional codebase you encounter, at a bank, a telecom, a startup, will follow some version of this pattern.</p><p>Refactor your Module 3 News Reader project: move its fetch logic into a repository and ViewModel with viewModelScope. It will behave the same but be structured like an app a company would actually ship, exactly what you want to show in interviews.</p>","18":"<p>In Module 3 you stored data with SharedPreferences and raw SQLite. SharedPreferences is perfect for small settings, but writing raw SQLite by hand is error-prone: one typo in an SQL string crashes at runtime. Google's <strong>Room</strong> library solves this, it sits on top of SQLite, checks your queries at compile time, and works beautifully with coroutines and ViewModels. For West African users who are frequently offline, a solid local database is not a luxury: it is what makes your app usable on a bus between Monrovia and Gbarnga with no signal.</p><h4>Room's Three Building Blocks</h4><ul><li><strong>Entity:</strong> a data class marked @Entity representing one table, for example, a Sale with id, itemName, amount, and date fields. Each property becomes a column.</li><li><strong>DAO (Data Access Object):</strong> an interface marked @Dao where you declare operations: @Insert to add, @Delete to remove, and @Query(\"SELECT * FROM sale ORDER BY date DESC\") for reads. Room generates the implementation and verifies the SQL when you build, errors surface immediately, not in users' hands.</li><li><strong>Database:</strong> an abstract class marked @Database listing entities and exposing DAOs. You create one instance for the whole app.</li></ul><h4>Reactive Reads with Flow</h4><p>A DAO query can return <strong>Flow&lt;List&lt;Sale&gt;&gt;</strong>, a stream that automatically emits new results whenever the table changes. The ViewModel collects it and updates state, so when a user records a sale, every screen showing sales updates instantly with no manual refresh code. Combined with Compose from 5.1, your UI stays effortlessly in sync with the database.</p><h4>The Offline-First Pattern</h4><p>Professional apps in low-connectivity markets follow offline-first design: the UI always reads from Room (fast, always available); a coroutine fetches fresh data from the API when a connection exists and writes it into Room; the UI updates automatically via Flow. The user sees cached news, prices, or records instantly, even offline, and fresh data appears when the network allows. This is how serious apps for African markets are built, from mobile banking to agricultural price trackers.</p><p>Upgrade your News Reader capstone: cache fetched articles in Room so the app works offline. With Compose, MVVM, coroutines, and Room together, you are now using the same modern stack as professional Android teams, and you have the vocabulary to prove it in any technical interview.</p>","19":"<p>This quiz tests <strong>Module 5: Jetpack Compose, Architecture & Room</strong>, @Composable functions, Column/Row/LazyColumn, state with remember and mutableStateOf, MVVM layers, coroutines with suspend and viewModelScope, and Room entities, DAOs, and Flow for offline-first apps.</p><p>You need <strong>70% to pass</strong>, with <strong>unlimited retries</strong>, these are also the most common Android interview topics, so every attempt is career preparation. If anything feels shaky, rebuild the counter and the Room-backed list from the lessons. You are learning the same stack professional teams use. Keep pushing!</p>","20":"<p>Everything you have stored so far lives on one phone. But the most valuable app ideas for Liberia, a marketplace connecting Red Light traders with buyers, a church community app, a school announcements platform, need users to share data and sign in from different devices. Traditionally that required building and paying for a server. <strong>Firebase</strong>, Google's backend platform, gives you those capabilities free at small scale, which is why it powers a huge share of African startups' first apps.</p><h4>The Services You Will Use Most</h4><ul><li><strong>Authentication:</strong> ready-made sign-in with email/password, phone number (very popular in West Africa where not everyone uses email), or Google accounts. A few lines of code replace weeks of building secure login yourself.</li><li><strong>Cloud Firestore:</strong> a cloud NoSQL database storing data as documents inside collections, for example a \"products\" collection where each document has name, price, and sellerId fields. Firestore syncs in real time: when a trader posts a product, every buyer's app updates within seconds. Crucially for Liberia, it has built-in <strong>offline persistence</strong>, reads and writes work offline and sync automatically when the connection returns.</li><li><strong>Cloud Messaging (FCM):</strong> free push notifications, so you can alert users about new messages or orders even when the app is closed.</li><li><strong>Crashlytics & Analytics:</strong> see real crashes and usage from the field, so you fix what actually breaks on the budget Android phones your users own.</li></ul><h4>How It Connects</h4><p>You register the app in the Firebase console, download the google-services.json config file into your project, and add the SDK dependencies. Reading data follows a familiar pattern: query a collection, receive snapshots in a listener or coroutine, update your ViewModel state, and Compose redraws. Your Module 5 architecture absorbs Firebase as just another data source behind the repository.</p><h4>Cost and Security Reality</h4><p>The free Spark plan comfortably covers learning and early apps (tens of thousands of reads per day). Two professional habits from day one: write <strong>security rules</strong> so users can only edit their own data, never leave a database in test mode in production, and structure data to minimize reads, since pricing scales with usage.</p><p>Your capstone begins now: choose a real community problem, and plan an app using Firebase Auth plus Firestore. In 6.2 we make it sustainable with monetization, and in 6.3 we turn it into your career launchpad.</p>","21":"<p>An app that solves a real problem deserves to sustain its developer. Monetization in West Africa has its own realities, few users own international credit cards, data is expensive, and trust must be earned, but developers across the region are building real incomes by matching the right model to the right audience. This lesson gives you the main models and the local strategy for each.</p><h4>Model 1: Advertising with AdMob</h4><p>Google AdMob shows ads inside your app and pays per view and click. Integration is straightforward: banner ads sit at screen edges, interstitials appear between actions, and rewarded ads give users something (extra features, content) for watching. Earnings per user in African markets are modest, so ads suit apps with large, engaged audiences, news, sports, entertainment. Golden rule: never let ads block core tasks, or users uninstall and your Play rating collapses.</p><h4>Model 2: In-App Purchases & Freemium</h4><p>Google Play Billing lets users buy premium features, the freemium pattern: the core app is free, power features cost money. Carrier billing (paying through phone credit) is available in many African countries and removes the credit-card barrier. Keep local buying power in mind: a $0.99-$2.99 price point converts far better than $9.99.</p><h4>Model 3: The B2B Model, Building for Businesses</h4><p>Often the strongest model in Liberia: the app is free for end users, and a business, church, school, or NGO pays you to build and maintain it. Monthly maintenance retainers ($50-$200 for small organizations) create predictable income. NGOs in particular commission data-collection and community-information apps and pay professional rates.</p><h4>Model 4: Direct Payments with Mobile Money</h4><p>For services sold inside your app to local users, integrate mobile money, MTN MoMo and Orange Money both offer developer APIs. Since Google Play policy requires Play Billing for digital in-app content, mobile money fits physical goods and real-world services (deliveries, tickets, school fees), exactly the app categories growing fastest in the region.</p><h4>Choosing Wisely</h4><p>Match model to audience: mass-market consumer app, ads plus freemium; tool for organizations, B2B contracts; marketplace or service app, transaction fees via mobile money. Add monetization after the app proves useful, never before.</p><p>Decide your capstone's model now and note it in your project plan, investors, clients, and interviewers all ask \"how does it sustain itself?\", and you will have an answer.</p>","22":"<p>This final lesson brings everything together: you will scope and ship a capstone app, then use it to open career doors. Every skill from the course has a place here, Kotlin, UI building, APIs, storage, architecture, Firebase, publishing, and the result is proof, visible to anyone on Google Play, that you can take an idea from nothing to a live product. In hiring terms, one published app outweighs pages of claims.</p><h4>Scoping the Capstone</h4><p>Pick a problem you personally understand from Liberian daily life, market price sharing, transport schedules, church or school announcements, a savings-group (susu) record keeper. Then cut the scope ruthlessly to a <strong>minimum viable product</strong>: three screens or fewer, one core feature done excellently. A polished, focused app beats an ambitious, broken one every time.</p><h4>The Professional Build Checklist</h4><ul><li><strong>Architecture:</strong> MVVM with ViewModels and a repository (Module 5.2).</li><li><strong>Data:</strong> Room for offline-first local storage (5.3), Firebase if users share data (6.1).</li><li><strong>UI:</strong> Material Design styling (Module 4), consistent colors, readable text, a clear primary action per screen.</li><li><strong>Resilience:</strong> loading, error, and empty states for every network operation; test in airplane mode.</li><li><strong>Quality:</strong> test on the smallest, cheapest device you can access; check Logcat for warnings; write unit tests for core logic.</li><li><strong>Ship it:</strong> sign, build the App Bundle, complete the listing, and publish (Module 4). Then gather feedback from ten real users and ship one improvement update, updates prove you maintain what you build.</li></ul><h4>Career Paths Open to You</h4><p><strong>Employment:</strong> banks, telecoms, startups, and agencies across West Africa hire Android developers; interviews focus on Kotlin, lifecycle, MVVM, coroutines, and past projects, all of which you now have. <strong>Freelancing:</strong> local businesses and NGOs commission apps; international clients hire through Upwork and similar platforms, where a published Play Store app is a decisive credential. <strong>Founding:</strong> your own app, monetized with 6.2's models, can become a product business, many African startups began exactly this way.</p><h4>Keep Growing</h4><p>Read other developers' code on GitHub, follow the official Android Developers documentation and codelabs, join African developer communities (GDG Monrovia and online Kotlin groups), and consider TIH's advanced courses on Firebase-deep-dive and cross-platform Flutter. You started this course installing Android Studio; you finish it as a developer with a live app, a modern stack, and three viable career paths. Congratulations, developer, now ship your capstone.</p>","23":"<p>The Final Assessment covers the <strong>whole journey</strong>: Kotlin fundamentals, activities and layouts, RecyclerView and Intents, storage and APIs, permissions, testing and Material Design, publishing, plus the modern stack from Modules 5 and 6, Jetpack Compose, MVVM, coroutines, Room, Firebase, and monetization strategy.</p><p>You need <strong>70% to pass</strong> to earn your TIH certificate, and <strong>unlimited retries</strong> mean a stumble costs nothing. You have written real Kotlin, shipped real screens, and planned a real capstone. Read carefully, trust your training, and claim your certificate, then go publish your app. Good luck, developer!</p>"};
